  "enhanced_information": "Adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) stimulates the adrenal cortex to produce cortisol. Tetracosactide, a synthetic derivative of ACTH, mimics its action and increases cortisol levels, confirming its similarity to ACTH. In contrast, Lanreotide and Pegvisomant do not have direct effects on adrenal function; Lanreotide primarily inhibits growth hormone and Pegvisomant blocks growth hormone receptors, neither of which relate to ACTH's role. Ganirelix, a GnRH antagonist, also does not influence adrenal cortex activity. Therefore, the only drug among the options that has properties similar to ACTH is Tetracosactide."
